    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • OMODA 4 Lands in Paris To Showcase Futuristic Styling, Looks Like a Discount Urus\nChicago Dealer Sells 2026 Chevrolet Corvette ZR1X Quail Silver Limited Edition for $430,000\n24 photos\nPhoto: Bring a Trailer/autoevolution\nHow would you like to spend $159,000 over MSRP on a brand-new Corvette ZR1X? Tempting, right? I'm only being half-sarcastic, because if you're a collector, this is exactly the type of Corvette you want to buy. A brand-new, limited-edition flagship spec with delivery miles.\nThing is, this is going to be a long-term investment, so make sure you're good with that. Otherwise, you might be disappointed trying to sell this car in a year or two. Prices will go down before going back up again, even on limited edition supercars with hypercar-like performance such as the ZR1X.\nIdeally, you'll want to sell this thing in like 2056 and make a few million bucks. Or whatever the equivalent of that will be three decades from now. What will work in your favor, as a collector, is that cars will be completely different in the future. Internal combustion will certainly be extinct 30 years from now, and people will look at the ZR1X the same way we might look at an all-original, numbers-matching, never driven, time capsule Boss 429 in today's market.\nBut in the meantime, scarcity won't be a factor and you'll have plenty of high performance models catching up to the ZR1X in terms of speed and acceleration. Almost certainly the next-generation C9 Corvette.\nNow, about the Quail Silver Limited Edition variant specifically, we saw a nearly identical car back in May that sold for $452,000 with an MSRP of $290,678. That one also had delivery miles. Meanwhile, this one here just sold for $430,000, and its original MSRP was $271,570. Seems pretty fair. Or at least it's within market parameters.\nOf course, anyone who's not a collector should avoid this limited edition spec like the plague, because you can get a ZR1X for way less than that. Around $330,000 should do it, on average - if you want it ASAP, that is. Meaning you'd have to win it during auction.\nIn terms of specs, this example is finished in Blade Silver Matte and comes with the Carbon Fiber Aero package (high-wing rear spoiler, dive planes, hood spoiler). Other visual highlights include the LED headlights, front and rear cameras, silver 20\" and 21\" forged wheels with Michelin rubber, plus the carbon-ceramic brakes with orange-finished calipers.\nPhoto: Bring a Trailer There's also a front axle lift system and you get the ZTK Performance suspension for good measure.\nInside is where you'll find the likes of the GT2 bucket seats (heated/ventilated), Sky Cool Gray and Medium Ash Gray leather upholstery with Habanero accents, dual-zone automatic climate control, cruise control, a Bose sound system, head-up display, and a limited edition badge between the seats that identifies this car as build #46 of a reported 100.\nAs for performance, the twin-turbocharged 5.5L LT7 V8 engine is working alongside an electric motor for a combined output of 1,250 horsepower and 973 lb-ft (1,320 Nm) of torque. Over a quarter-mile, this is an 8-second car when running at its best.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nchevrolet corvette zr1x Corvette ZR1X Chevrolet Corvette Chevrolet Corvette chevrolet corvette zr1x quail silver auction\nAbout the author: Sergiu Tudose\nSergiu got to experience both American and European car \"scenes\" at an early age (his father drove a Ford Fiesta XR2 supermini in the 80s). After spending over 15 years at local and international auto publications, he's starting to appreciate comfort behind the wheel more than raw power and acceleration. Full profile\nUP NEXT LEGO Ideas Alfa Romeo Montreal Mirrors the Best Italian Car That Launched at the Worst Possible Time",
